    44.1\n2002                  35.3    0.0        64.7\n2004                  44.1    0.0        55.9\n2006                  66.7    6.1        27.3\n2008                  57.1    0.0        42.9\n2010                  35.1    2.7        62.2\n2012                  69.7    6.1        24.2\n2014                  33.3    0.0        66.7\n2016                  35.3    0.0        64.7\n2018                  62.9    5.7        31.4\n2020                  42.9    0.0        57.1\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# positive means Republicans won more seats, negative means Democrats did\nparty_gap = seat_share[\"REPUBLICAN\"] - seat_share[\"DEMOCRAT\"]\n\ntop_years = party_gap.abs().nlargest(8).index.sort_values()\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(9, 5))\nplt.bar(top_years.astype(str), party_gap.loc[top_years])\nplt.axhline(0, color=\"black\", linewidth=1)\n\nplt.title(\"Largest Differences in Senate Seat Share\")\nplt.xlabel(\"Year\")\nplt.ylabel(\"Republican minus Democrat (%)\")\nplt.xticks(rotation=45)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-21T20:10:18.867803Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-21T20:10:18.868097Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-21T20:10:19.094249Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-21T20:10:18.868068Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-21T20:10:19.093279Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 900x500 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":10},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"*Senate results swing a lot from one cycle to the next because only about a third of the seats are up each time. Democrats had their best year in 2012, winning 23 of 33 races (69.7%), while Republicans had their best year two years later in 2014 with 24 of 36 (66.7%). Other parties never won more than 2 seats in a year, mostly independents such as Bernie Sanders and Angus King. In 2020 Republicans won 20 seats and Democrats won 15, with Democrats taking both Georgia runoffs.*","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Conclusion","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The analysis shows clear changes in U.S. elections between 1976 and 2020. Presidential vote share moved back and forth between the two main parties, with the Republican high in 1984 (58.5%) and the Democratic high in 2008 (52.8%), while third parties mainly made an impact in 1980, 1992 and 1996. The biggest change is in split ticket voting, which fell from 54.5% in 1984 to 0% in 2016, meaning states now almost always vote for the same party for President and Senate. At the same time the median presidential margin roughly doubled from 1992 to 2016, so fewer states are close contests. Senate seat share swings sharply between cycles, as seen in the shift from 2012 to 2014. Overall, the results point to a more polarized electorate where party loyalty matters more than individual candidates.","metadata":{}}]}